WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-CA

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.600
What if writer's block isn't actually a block,&nbsp;
but rather a sign that you're on the verge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.960
a breakthrough? Most writers, whenever they hit&nbsp;
one of these blocks, try to discipline their way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.160
through it with daily word counts, timers, and&nbsp;
putting your butt in the chair. But that doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:23.040
work. And I know because I tried it all during a&nbsp;
year-long block that nearly made me quit writing.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.320
What finally got me through it was not&nbsp;
more discipline. It was the opposite.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:34.240
So in this video I'm going to show you what I&nbsp;
think writer's block actually is, why discipline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.200
makes it worse, and the daily practice that&nbsp;
helped me break through it. And to do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:45.360
I'm going to tell you a quick story about how I&nbsp;
broke through my biggest period of writer's block.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.880
This was during the COVID-19 pandemic and I&nbsp;
was living alone in the Korean countryside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.960
My university had us teaching online and I was&nbsp;
using my downtime to work on the great foreigner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:04.160
novel. On paper, these were ideal conditions.&nbsp;
I had a steady paycheck and plenty of solitude,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.440
a stack of books, and my typewriter. I&nbsp;
could essentially do my job in my pajamas.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.680
And so, despite these conditions,&nbsp;
I was completely blocked up. I did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:22.560
all the productivity stuff — I sat my&nbsp;
butt in my chair from 7:30 to 12:00,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:28.240
but I just couldn't write. To get through this&nbsp;
block, I really doubled down on the route of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:35.200
discipline. I thought the problem was me and my&nbsp;
habits. I was reading the James Clear book and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:41.920
really trying to figure out how to force myself&nbsp;
to write words even when I didn't feel like it.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.280
But what I found is that this approach&nbsp;
makes things worse. What started happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.560
was I was building up resentment toward the work&nbsp;
because I didn't want to be there. I didn't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:58.560
to continue on. Even though I had set myself&nbsp;
this grand project of writing a great novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:04.400
I hated doing the daily work — until one day I&nbsp;
had finally had it. I packed up my typewriter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.040
put it in the closet, and said: I've had it. This&nbsp;
is enough. I don't want to look at this anymore.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:19.520
So, if that was a Friday, then it was only two&nbsp;
nights later — Sunday night — I'm lying in bed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:26.720
and I just get hit with this wave of creative&nbsp;
inspiration. I start getting all these ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:31.440
about the next project that I want to take&nbsp;
on, one that I'm genuinely excited about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:37.280
Thrilled about. And the ideas feel like&nbsp;
they're coming out of nowhere. The idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:44.000
was to take my teaching online, and that&nbsp;
eventually became this YouTube channel.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.520
It was so out of the blue. You have to think:&nbsp;
I'm a guy who's writing on a typewriter. At that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:57.840
phase in my life I was very anti-technology.&nbsp;
I had apps and strategies to lock myself out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.640
of social media. I didn't have much&nbsp;
social media. And come to think of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:10.160
at that time I don't even think I had an&nbsp;
account on YouTube. So the idea of building a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:16.400
personal brand and learning about video — are you&nbsp;
kidding me? — all of this was so foreign to me.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.440
But it was only when I packed up that&nbsp;
project and took it out of sight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:27.760
and stopped forcing myself to tend to&nbsp;
this goal that a previous version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:36.160
me had set — the version who wanted to be a&nbsp;
novelist and write a great novel — only when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:44.080
I could finally take that off my table and put&nbsp;
it away did I get hit with this wave of ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:50.400
that at the time seemed quite alien to me,&nbsp;
but has now sustained my creative practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:57.360
for five years. It was something that was truly&nbsp;
me. It just didn't match who I thought I was.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.560
And I have to say, this wave of creative&nbsp;
inspiration was almost overwhelming. At a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.760
point it was like: okay, enough — I want to think&nbsp;
about this tomorrow. I couldn't turn it off. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:16.960
was just so much energy in this train of thought&nbsp;
that I didn't really know how to harness it.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:22.480
This has only happened to me one other time,&nbsp;
and it was actually more recently — about three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.520
months ago. I did a 10-day meditation retreat.&nbsp;
I've been doing these for a number of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:34.320
so I've probably done 12 or 15 of them by&nbsp;
this point. But on this most recent retreat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:39.440
I had a similar experience, and I think in&nbsp;
this case it happened for a different reason.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.320
In the tradition of meditation that I practice,&nbsp;
whenever we do these retreats, it's silence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:52.880
No phone, no speaking, no eye contact. So even&nbsp;
though there are other people there, you're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.000
engaging with others at all. You're completely&nbsp;
self-contained. Unless you need to talk to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.040
manager about the food or a teacher about your&nbsp;
practice, you're not engaging with other students.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.400
So there I am in the Korean countryside, sitting&nbsp;
in essentially a broom closet — that's the size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:16.800
of it, it's a meditation cell. And somewhere&nbsp;
around day five or six of this 10-day retreat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:22.320
I similarly started getting hit with these&nbsp;
waves and waves of creative ideas. In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:27.920
it was about things I wanted to do with the&nbsp;
YouTube channel and the website writerscience.com.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:30.720
The point here is not about the ideas themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.000
but about the conditions that gave rise&nbsp;
to them. So what do these two situations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.480
have in common? And how can you set&nbsp;
those same conditions in your life?

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:48.000
To do that, I need to give you a piece&nbsp;
of psychoanalytic theory that completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:54.000
changed the way I understand creativity and&nbsp;
the unconscious mind — because ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.520
that's where your creativity&nbsp;
comes from. The unconscious.

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:02.720
The basic idea in psychoanalysis&nbsp;
is that we are not a whole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:09.200
coherent self — just one self — but&nbsp;
actually, well, Freud would have said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:17.040
that we're more like two: the conscious mind&nbsp;
and the unconscious mind. Like an iceberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:24.880
you know — we only know the tip, but there's&nbsp;
a deeper layer called the unconscious.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:32.160
But there's another thinker named Lacan who takes&nbsp;
this a little bit further, and I think this gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.120
us a really good model for understanding how&nbsp;
creativity works and where it comes from. Lacan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:44.400
says that the self — human subjectivity — is&nbsp;
actually made up of three different layers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:55.280
or registers. He calls them the Real, the&nbsp;
Symbolic, and the Imaginary. The Real goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:00.480
to the Imaginary, the Imaginary to the&nbsp;
Symbolic, and the Symbolic back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:07.600
Real. And in the middle there is this force called&nbsp;
*jouissance*, which bubbles up through the Real.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:09.680
All right, so what does any of that mean?

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:15.760
The Imaginary layer of yourself is who you&nbsp;
imagine yourself to be. It's the self you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:21.760
see when you look in the mirror in your mind.&nbsp;
It's the story you tell yourself about who you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:27.840
are and who you're supposed to be. The&nbsp;
Symbolic is your social self — the set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:34.080
of masks and personas that you put on&nbsp;
to navigate the world and satisfy the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:39.120
demands that other people put on you. It's your&nbsp;
job, your relationships, and your reputation.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:46.560
But the Real — to put it simply — is&nbsp;
who you really are behind closed doors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:52.400
It's what makes you uniquely you. You could&nbsp;
call this your soul, or your true self,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:58.800
or however you want to put it. It's the&nbsp;
thing that makes you *you* like nobody else.

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:04.000
But here's the thing: we have these layers&nbsp;
on top of it. We have an Imaginary self,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:12.720
like an ego — a story we tell ourselves about&nbsp;
who we are that has a kind of consistency.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:18.400
If somebody asks you who you are, you tell&nbsp;
a story about it, and it has a beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:22.560
middle, and end and a coherent whole.&nbsp;
The Real isn't like that at all. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:28.640
incoherent. It can't be contained.&nbsp;
It exceeds all bounds and forms.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:32.880
So you have the Imaginary — the self you see&nbsp;
when you look in the mirror — and then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:38.240
have all these masks you use to navigate the&nbsp;
world, your job and your relationships. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:45.600
all of these are piled on top of the Real,&nbsp;
holding it down and containing its excesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:52.880
so that you don't screw up socially and so&nbsp;
that you don't lose the sense of self you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:58.400
cultivated. These things are blocking&nbsp;
you from the source of your creativity.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:04.400
And I think that's what was happening in my&nbsp;
story. Whenever I was packing up my typewriter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:12.160
I was not just clearing room off of my desk.&nbsp;
In a sense, I was killing the Imaginary part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:19.120
of myself. I'd been carrying this vision of&nbsp;
myself as a novelist for years and years — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:25.920
don't even know where I got it — but I was at&nbsp;
a point in my life when that ego was no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:33.200
in service. I know that because in living out&nbsp;
that fantasy, I was not happy. I was miserable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:42.880
I didn't feel free. I didn't have a real outlet&nbsp;
to express who I am. And it was driving me crazy.

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:50.240
It was only when I packed it up that I was&nbsp;
able to unleash all this pent-up energy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:55.200
It came through. And then I built up a new&nbsp;
image of myself — one who's here teaching on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.200 --> 00:10:00.400
YouTube — interested in everything that&nbsp;
comes with this completely new genre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:08.560
this completely new medium. It only came into&nbsp;
the world when I killed that part of myself.

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:14.880
The same goes for the Symbolic, the social&nbsp;
persona. It was only when I was on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:22.480
meditation retreat and separated from all my&nbsp;
relationships that I was able to access this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:28.560
new wave of creativity. Of course, these things&nbsp;
are necessary — you're not going to just dispense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:36.400
with them. But sometimes they can calcify.&nbsp;
If we don't allow the energy from the Real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:42.800
to come up and invigorate these conceptions&nbsp;
of self and these symbolic relationships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:50.080
then we can become stuck. And I&nbsp;
think that is what writer's block is.

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:58.240
It's when the energy from *jouissance* is&nbsp;
not able to come up and inform the kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:05.280
of persona and the fantasies that you use to&nbsp;
shape yourself in the world. In both cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:13.040
the thing that unleashed this creativity was not&nbsp;
a new discipline routine. It was the ability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:21.040
in one way or another, to get out of the way&nbsp;
— to loosen my grip on these constructs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:27.280
allow them to be rejuvenated through this&nbsp;
energy that's already pent up inside of me.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:34.160
Although this might seem pretty inspirational&nbsp;
and easy to do at a theoretical level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:41.120
it's not easy in practice. We're actually&nbsp;
very attached to these things. In my case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:48.880
I would not have easily dispensed with the idea&nbsp;
of myself as a novelist. And similarly with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:54.640
masks we wear in public — to lose that façade,&nbsp;
we have that expression (at least in Asia):&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.640 --> 00:12:01.120
to lose face. It's very painful and&nbsp;
embarrassing to lose face, because that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:07.120
means you are letting down and disappointing&nbsp;
the expectations that others put on you.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:16.720
But it's also painful to release the energy&nbsp;
through the Real into these layers — because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:23.120
as I said, when I was lying there in bed, these&nbsp;
were exciting ideas I was getting flooded with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:28.480
but it was almost too much to bear.&nbsp;
It's very overwhelming. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:33.920
that's what these layers are for. They're&nbsp;
protective. These are the floodgates that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:40.720
hold back the tidal waters. But the cost&nbsp;
of that protection is your creative work.

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:45.680
Which brings me to the point that runs&nbsp;
underneath this entire video: **you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:52.720
to let yourself die in order for your work to&nbsp;
live.** Real creativity means letting go of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.720 --> 00:13:00.480
control — especially control over who you imagine&nbsp;
yourself to be and who others expect you to be.

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:05.680
So if discipline is not the answer to your&nbsp;
writer's block, then what is? And how do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:10.640
develop a relationship to the Real and&nbsp;
to your unconscious without forcing it?

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:13.760
If you've been developing a writing&nbsp;
practice for any amount of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:18.400
you've probably heard of all kinds of productivity&nbsp;
routines — from people like Jerry Seinfeld,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:24.240
who locks himself in a room for whatever it&nbsp;
is, one hour a day, with a rule where during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:30.480
that time he doesn't need to write, but he's not&nbsp;
allowed to do anything else. No phone, no books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:36.080
no music. You just sit there until something&nbsp;
comes. This story gets passed around as advice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:39.760
but has anybody ever actually tried&nbsp;
it? Well, I've tried it, folks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:46.000
and let me tell you — it doesn't work. I think&nbsp;
it's more myth than actual practical advice.

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:54.960
It probably works for a certain kind of low-value&nbsp;
writing. For emails or reports, I guess it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.960 --> 00:14:01.520
work to have a forcing function that makes you do&nbsp;
it. But for creative work that's uniquely yours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:06.240
this does not work. Because look at what&nbsp;
you're doing: you're sitting in a room and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:12.400
trying to force this to come up. And the&nbsp;
unconscious just does not work that way.

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:14.240
All this language around discipline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:18.000
when you think about it through this&nbsp;
frame of the unconscious — you realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:23.360
that it's just a substitute for forcing the&nbsp;
unconscious. And you simply cannot do that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:27.680
It doesn't work. It's your unconscious&nbsp;
calling the shots around here, not you.

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:45.600
The best way to understand this is with the&nbsp;
metaphor of the elephant. You've got an elephant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.600 --> 00:15:05.920
and then you've got a rider on top. The rider is&nbsp;
sitting on top of the elephant holding the reins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:11.360
and they think they're in control. The&nbsp;
elephant might have good humor — it might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:15.840
take some suggestions if you guide it&nbsp;
this way and that. But if the elephant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:21.520
decides that it wants to go in a certain&nbsp;
direction, what are you going to do?

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:28.400
I think this comes up a lot whenever we&nbsp;
pick long-term projects. You decide five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:34.160
years ago that you're going to write some&nbsp;
fantasy novel or some collection of essays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:39.840
and suddenly you feel blocked. Well, I think&nbsp;
that's because you're up there trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:44.880
push the elephant in one direction, but&nbsp;
the elephant is done with that. Every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:51.760
blocked rider is doing the same thing: they&nbsp;
picked some destination long ago — some book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:59.440
project or idea of self — and they're trying to&nbsp;
force the elephant to go in that direction. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:04.800
whenever you say you have writer's block, I&nbsp;
think it's because you've picked a project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:10.160
that the elephant no longer wants to comply with.&nbsp;
So here you are sitting on top of the elephant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:14.560
yanking at the reins, trying to control&nbsp;
it, and wondering why nothing's happening.

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:21.280
But what if the elephant actually knew better&nbsp;
than the rider? What if the elephant had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:28.480
rider's best intentions in mind and&nbsp;
could see a little bit farther? Would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:34.480
you loosen your grip a little bit, trust it,&nbsp;
and let it take you where it's trying to go?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:40.400
And what if the place where it was trying to&nbsp;
go was better than the one you had in mind?

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:46.800
The unconscious is working&nbsp;
on your behalf all the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:52.640
even when you sleep. Scientists and&nbsp;
mathematicians call it the night shift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:58.560
because there have been so many cases when&nbsp;
a researcher will be working on a problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:05.760
then go to sleep, and the solution&nbsp;
comes to them in the riddle of a dream.

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:10.480
The famous example is the French&nbsp;
scientist Kekulé. He was working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:15.760
on benzene — he needed to know its shape,&nbsp;
and he just couldn't figure it out. So one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:22.480
day Kekulé goes to sleep and has a dream&nbsp;
of the mythical snake eating its own tail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:30.320
the Ouroboros. He wakes up and suddenly&nbsp;
realizes: *aha, it's a ring.* Kekulé&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:36.080
solved this very important scientific&nbsp;
problem not at his desk, but in his sleep.

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:40.880
But this doesn't just work for scientists and&nbsp;
researchers. Anybody who's played an instrument&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:46.480
knows how this works. If you play guitar or&nbsp;
piano — chime in in the comments if this has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:56.720
ever happened to you — you start learning a&nbsp;
new song on day one at zero percent. You pick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.720 --> 00:18:03.920
a section and you practice and you practice,&nbsp;
and after about an hour you get exhausted and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:08.720
hit your limit. You're not making any more&nbsp;
marginal progress. So you set it down and go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:19.840
to sleep. Let's say on day one you went from 0%&nbsp;
to 15% — 15% of the song you can now play. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:27.360
the following day when you practice&nbsp;
that song again, do you start at 15%?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:36.720
No. You start at like 25%, 30%. You make&nbsp;
progress in the song *in your sleep.*

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:41.040
I don't mean that you're learning notes&nbsp;
you haven't studied before — I'm talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:47.440
about the dexterity to perform the song. It&nbsp;
becomes a lot easier to play and a lot more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:54.000
natural. That's because the muscle memory gets&nbsp;
consolidated and streamlined in your sleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:11.840
You were working on a problem during the day with&nbsp;
your conscious mind, and then your conscious mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:16.960
handed it over to your subconscious, which did&nbsp;
its own procedures on it. That's the night shift.

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:22.960
Why do you suppose it is that the unconscious&nbsp;
speaks to us in dreams with mythological snake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:27.520
shapes instead of just telling us the&nbsp;
answer in plain English? Simply enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:33.040
it's because the unconscious is older than&nbsp;
language. Language came around only about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.040 --> 00:19:38.480
10,000 years ago, but the unconscious has been&nbsp;
around for millions of years — as long as Homo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:44.480
sapiens have been around. The unconscious&nbsp;
is the operating system for an animal. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:50.240
an animal did not have an unconscious, it would&nbsp;
be a plant. The unconscious existed long before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:56.480
we had words to think with, and it still&nbsp;
runs underneath every thought that you have.

00:19:56.480 --> 00:20:03.360
So whenever you can't reach an idea with this&nbsp;
newest part of your brain — the neocortex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:10.160
the language part — the unconscious is still&nbsp;
there working in the background. It just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:18.160
speaks in a different register: in images,&nbsp;
sensations, dreams, hunches, and itches.

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:23.120
So if you're feeling blocked, this is what&nbsp;
your elephant has been trying to tell you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:32.880
The unconscious is not your adversary. It's not&nbsp;
some chaotic force that you have to manage. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:37.520
constantly working on your behalf, solving&nbsp;
problems that the neocortex, this new kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:46.320
on the block, couldn't solve itself. It's going&nbsp;
to be composing the sentence you couldn't finish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:51.760
yesterday, and connecting the two ideas&nbsp;
you'd never thought to connect together.

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:57.680
This, I think, is how I've learned to trust&nbsp;
the elephant — my unconscious. I've learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.680 --> 00:21:03.760
through experience that it gives you what&nbsp;
you need precisely when you need it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:09.600
the times in my life when I've just followed&nbsp;
it — call it intuition or grace or whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:16.320
it might be — it has led me somewhere&nbsp;
better than I, in my previous state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:22.400
could have ever imagined. We use the&nbsp;
best of our knowledge to make our plans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.400 --> 00:21:26.880
but sometimes those plans are lousy because&nbsp;
you don't know what you don't know. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:34.400
unconscious is looking out for you and it's&nbsp;
trying to tell you something important.

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:40.240
So if you're blocked on this project&nbsp;
and it's just not letting you through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.240 --> 00:21:46.400
maybe just consider: I'm all for goals. I don't&nbsp;
think there's anything wrong with trying to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:52.400
more disciplined in your life. But the problem&nbsp;
is taking a discipline approach to what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.400 --> 00:22:00.880
essentially a creative endeavor. If you want&nbsp;
to be an artist, you need to learn to listen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:07.440
to your unconscious and follow it wherever it's&nbsp;
trying to lead you. You cannot summon it. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.440 --> 00:22:13.040
can't give it a deadline, and you can't bully&nbsp;
it. You are not going to David Goggins your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:19.200
way through this book. But the good news is&nbsp;
you don't have to. It's already on your side.

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:22.720
So if the unconscious is working on your behalf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.720 --> 00:22:27.200
the only question left is: how do&nbsp;
you give it the room to deliver?

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:40.080
My answer is freewriting. Here's a way to&nbsp;
think about it. If you imagine yourself as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:55.600
being made up of those three different layers, it&nbsp;
actually looks kind of like a volcano. Below you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.600 --> 00:23:05.760
inside, is magma — pressurized and full&nbsp;
of energy, with no shape and no language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:13.120
This is the source of your creativity. Freewriting&nbsp;
is the act of pulling a little bit of that magma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:21.520
to the surface every day. You sit down and just&nbsp;
write whatever comes to mind. No filter, no plan.

00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:26.160
You've probably heard something like this before,&nbsp;
but let me clarify a few things. When you do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:33.040
freewriting, it's really important not to put&nbsp;
any pressure on yourself to produce something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.040 --> 00:23:39.440
meaningful. You're not sitting down and doing&nbsp;
freewriting and then trying to adapt it into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:45.440
newsletter. You're not freewriting as the first&nbsp;
phase of your essay. I'm not even talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:52.640
first drafts here. What I'm talking about&nbsp;
is dredging up the Real — pulling up this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.640 --> 00:24:01.440
creative magma within you and just getting&nbsp;
it into language, into a shape. A lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:07.040
which is going to be totally trash. It's not&nbsp;
even "trash" in the usual sense, because you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:13.600
can't apply qualitative judgments to it&nbsp;
— something is only good or bad against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.600 --> 00:24:18.720
something you were trying to achieve. If&nbsp;
you're using language like good, bad, better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:25.760
worse, you've already missed the point. The&nbsp;
product of this freewriting is not meaningful.

00:24:26.800 --> 00:24:33.040
If there's a goal to it, the goal is just to&nbsp;
open up a vent. Because when you're blocked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:38.720
it's because these things are&nbsp;
blocking this. What you want is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.720 --> 00:24:42.960
to open a vent and let a little&nbsp;
bit of this juice out every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:53.680
so it can energize and rejuvenate the images&nbsp;
and symbols we use to fashion ourselves and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.680 --> 00:24:58.560
our understanding and our model of the&nbsp;
world. You're just letting it breathe.

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:06.240
When the magma hits the page — when what&nbsp;
is bubbling within you gets translated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:10.480
into language — then it cools into a&nbsp;
shape. Almost like you're dropping the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.480 --> 00:25:16.080
magma into a bucket. When you drop magma&nbsp;
into a bucket, you don't decide what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:20.960
shape is. It's just going to take whatever&nbsp;
shape it takes based on the interaction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:20.960 --> 00:25:26.640
the original source material and the new&nbsp;
conditions in the environment. That's it.

00:25:26.640 --> 00:25:32.640
Now, you as the excavator of this magma can&nbsp;
look at that final shape — and of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:36.240
you're not going to try to give this&nbsp;
to somebody else; it's not meaningful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:43.120
it's not useful, it's just magma. But it&nbsp;
might solidify into a shape that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:50.080
then refine into something that another&nbsp;
person might find useful or beautiful.

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:56.240
When you do this freewriting, it's going to take&nbsp;
whatever form it takes. There's nothing you can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:56.240 --> 00:26:02.960
about that. You can't will it to be prettier. You&nbsp;
can't will it to be more useful. You just drop it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:09.360
into the bucket, drop it into language, and let&nbsp;
it cool down. Whatever you get is what you get.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:15.840
But that's fine, because freewriting is not meant&nbsp;
to be meaningful. It's not for anybody — and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:23.520
includes you. What comes up is going to look&nbsp;
like gobbledygook, and that's perfectly fine.

00:26:23.520 --> 00:26:29.040
If you were a painter, this would be the&nbsp;
equivalent of just moving paint around on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:34.320
the canvas. This isn't an early sketch of&nbsp;
a painting you're going to do. It's not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:35.360 --> 00:26:40.480
study. Sometimes painters will do that&nbsp;
— little sketches, experiments with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:40.480 --> 00:26:45.440
paint. That's not what we're doing. We are&nbsp;
*playing* with paint at this stage. That's it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.440 --> 00:26:52.800
Magma has no coherent structure. It's just full of&nbsp;
energy. The whole point of this practice is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:52.800 --> 00:27:01.040
to get that energy out of the underground and&nbsp;
put it into a form cool enough for you to hold.

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:08.800
Once the magma's cooled — which might be moments&nbsp;
later, or hours later, or days later — then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:15.760
analytical mind can come online and you can&nbsp;
read what you wrote and start to sort it out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.760 --> 00:27:22.640
You're going to find that a lot of it is dross,&nbsp;
and that's fine. But you're also going to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:28.240
certain bits that have a bit of a shine to them,&nbsp;
or what some people say has *energy* — there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.240 --> 00:27:38.880
something to it, something you see, something&nbsp;
meaningful, something true, something that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:38.880 --> 00:27:44.800
can work with. As you work with it, you're going&nbsp;
to start to shape it into something you can share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:52.960
with others — something that's either useful&nbsp;
like a tool or beautiful like a sculpture.

00:27:53.840 --> 00:28:02.400
This kind of analysis is where your craft lives.&nbsp;
That's when the manuscript, the newsletter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:07.840
the essay, the script — that's when it gets&nbsp;
made. But the sequence here is really important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:12.480
because you can't sculpt magma. You've&nbsp;
got to let it cool down. You've got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:19.440
let these ideas first percolate inside&nbsp;
of you. You can't force this to come up.

00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:24.640
Whenever you do these freewriting sessions,&nbsp;
you just get whatever you're given. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.640 --> 00:28:29.200
Greeks wrote about this as being given&nbsp;
gifts from the gods — it's as though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.200 --> 00:28:35.440
it's coming *through* you. You can't choose&nbsp;
what comes up. You've just got to allow it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:35.440 --> 00:28:41.680
to come up and then review it. It's great&nbsp;
if you do this in note-taking software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:41.680 --> 00:28:50.160
because you can use a random note function&nbsp;
to resurface old freewriting scraps. You can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:56.640
really discover things months or years later&nbsp;
that you didn't see the day you wrote them.

00:28:56.640 --> 00:29:02.240
Even if you've never actually done this writing&nbsp;
practice, you already know the difference I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:07.360
talking about. Think about the difference&nbsp;
between two different kinds of conversation.

00:29:08.160 --> 00:29:13.120
Sometimes you have a conversation with somebody&nbsp;
and you're just in your head. You're a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:13.120 --> 00:29:19.040
self-conscious, a little dry-mouthed — your&nbsp;
ideas aren't really coming and they're pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:25.920
clunky when they do. And if you do hit any kind&nbsp;
of coherent patch, what you're doing is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:31.680
rehearsing something you've said before. The&nbsp;
other person, whenever this happens, usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:37.520
gets a sense like they're being talked *at* rather&nbsp;
than talked *with*. That's a bad conversation.

00:29:37.520 --> 00:29:45.120
But then sometimes you just hit a topic that you&nbsp;
really care about and you stop monitoring what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:45.120 --> 00:29:51.760
you're saying. The ideas come pouring out&nbsp;
of you, and even though they're new ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.760 --> 00:29:59.280
they seem to cohere — as though they're coming&nbsp;
up from a unified whole. It's not an old idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:04.160
you've rehearsed. It's not a familiar argument.&nbsp;
As teachers, we have these kinds of teaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.160 --> 00:30:08.640
routines: you say this, then you say that, then&nbsp;
you insert a joke here. That's not what we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:15.440
talking about. Sometimes in a conversation&nbsp;
you just hit your stride and the ideas start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:15.440 --> 00:30:22.720
pouring out one by one, and they have a kind of&nbsp;
energy. It's really exciting when this happens.

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:36.320
And every time this happens, it's not because you&nbsp;
planned it to be this way — it's because you got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:42.080
out of the way. You weren't generating&nbsp;
and judging. You were just letting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:42.080 --> 00:30:47.840
magma rise. Because your unconscious is&nbsp;
always working for you in the background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:47.840 --> 00:30:54.880
it will give you sometimes these ideas that&nbsp;
are entirely coherent, thought through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.880 --> 00:31:03.120
easy to articulate, well-argued, substantiated&nbsp;
by evidence and experience — the whole package.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.120 --> 00:31:07.680
That happens in conversation. And that's&nbsp;
exactly what I'm talking about when I talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:15.040
about the purpose of freewriting. It's the&nbsp;
practice that makes that second mode reliable.

00:31:15.040 --> 00:31:20.880
Of course, you can never force it. But&nbsp;
by regularly practicing freewriting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:28.080
you give it an opportunity to happen. Most&nbsp;
of what comes up is trash — probably 90%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:33.360
of it. This is magma we're talking about. But&nbsp;
the 10% that isn't is going to be better than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:33.360 --> 00:31:38.400
anything you could have planned. That's how&nbsp;
the unconscious works. It knows who you are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:43.520
It knows what you're working on and it's&nbsp;
always working for you in the background.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.520 --> 00:31:47.280
You've just got to get out of the way&nbsp;
and give it a chance to step forward.

00:31:47.280 --> 00:31:53.200
The way to do this is to write the raw material&nbsp;
first and then bring your intellect online to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:59.840
do the shaping and refining. Meaning in these&nbsp;
freewriting sessions is what you end up with —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:59.840 --> 00:32:06.320
it's not what you start with. All you need to do&nbsp;
is show up daily, open the vent, let the magma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:06.320 --> 00:32:13.120
cool, and trust that the version of you that's&nbsp;
trying to come through has something worth saying.

00:32:13.120 --> 00:32:16.480
So there you have it. You're not&nbsp;
stuck. There's just something bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:21.600
in you that's trying to come up. All&nbsp;
you've got to do is get out of its way.

